WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.620
Can an attacker get into our
systems without the password?

00:00:04.450 --> 00:00:06.820
A stolen session cookie does exactly that.

00:00:07.005 --> 00:00:10.155
It skips the multi-factor
check the user already passed.

00:00:11.617 --> 00:00:13.607
First, a script reads the cookie.

00:00:14.002 --> 00:00:16.992
An attacker injects
JavaScript into a page,

00:00:17.342 --> 00:00:21.692
and that script can read any
cookie without the HttpOnly flag.

00:00:22.067 --> 00:00:25.797
Nothing looks wrong, and the
multi-factor check was already passed.

00:00:26.177 --> 00:00:28.427
Consider the HttpOnly flag,

00:00:28.857 --> 00:00:31.067
so JavaScript cannot touch the cookie.

00:00:32.117 --> 00:00:34.787
Second, someone catches
the cookie on its way.

00:00:35.062 --> 00:00:37.352
A phishing proxy or plain HTTP

00:00:37.862 --> 00:00:39.592
lets the attacker copy it.

00:00:39.762 --> 00:00:41.932
Wi-Fi sniffing works the same way.

00:00:42.117 --> 00:00:44.167
Consider phishing-resistant MFA,

00:00:44.577 --> 00:00:47.047
the Secure flag and HSTS.

00:00:48.117 --> 00:00:50.827
Third and fourth,
the session itself is abused.

00:00:51.272 --> 00:00:51.962
With fixation,

00:00:52.332 --> 00:00:56.842
we hand out a session ID that the attacker
guessed first, because it was weak.

00:00:57.132 --> 00:00:58.382
With a forged request,

00:00:58.832 --> 00:01:03.042
the browser sends the cookie along with
something the user never asked for.

00:01:03.232 --> 00:01:07.302
Suggested: strong randomness,
a new ID at every login,

00:01:07.472 --> 00:01:10.362
SameSite Lax and CSRF tokens.

00:01:11.483 --> 00:01:13.953
Fifth, a local AI coding agent.

00:01:14.183 --> 00:01:16.603
It can read the browser's
profile files directly,

00:01:16.603 --> 00:01:19.693
with no JavaScript and no network.

00:01:20.118 --> 00:01:23.888
HttpOnly, Secure and
SameSite do not help here.

00:01:24.118 --> 00:01:29.248
Consider a sandboxed agent, device-bound
sessions and an outbound allowlist.

00:01:30.217 --> 00:01:31.427
What good looks like.

00:01:31.712 --> 00:01:34.662
Defence in depth,
several layers that work together.

00:01:34.992 --> 00:01:38.602
HttpOnly stops script theft,
but not interception.

00:01:39.077 --> 00:01:40.307
Secure stops interception,

00:01:40.857 --> 00:01:41.847
but not fixation.

00:01:42.397 --> 00:01:44.247
SameSite stops forged requests,

00:01:44.697 --> 00:01:46.047
but not injected scripts.

00:01:46.522 --> 00:01:48.312
The weakest link breaks the chain.

00:01:50.130 --> 00:01:51.340
A suggested first step.

00:01:51.625 --> 00:01:53.975
Ask the security lead for five numbers.

00:01:54.340 --> 00:01:57.470
The share of cookies
with HttpOnly and Secure,

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:01.410
and the share of sign-ins
with phishing-resistant MFA.

00:02:02.015 --> 00:02:06.045
Also open XSS and CSRF
findings from testing.

00:02:07.717 --> 00:02:10.307
A stolen cookie is a stolen identity.

00:02:10.592 --> 00:02:12.982
Layer the controls, and track the numbers.

